Notebook[{
Cell["PadicPeriodLength", "ObjectName",ExpressionUUID->"4b64ae3d-6a88-427e-ae97-cd05fd7deb9b"],

Cell["\<\
PadicPeriodLength[r,p] gives the minimal eventual period of an exact rational \
in prime base p without constructing its digit cycle. Terminating expansions, \
including zero, have period 0; negative integers have period 1. \
PadicPeriodLength[s] uses a PadicRational object's base; \
PadicPeriodLength[s,p] uses p. Lazy and finite objects have no direct period \
form; use PadicPeriodLength[Normal[s],p] for a lazy value.\
\>", "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"41be4108-0d4b-4a0f-9992-46db9e02f5fb"],

Cell["\<\
For a normalized reduced fraction u/v with v>1, the eventual period is \
MultiplicativeOrder[p,v]. Positive integers and zero terminate; negative \
integers repeat the digit p-1.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"4f3928df-9f95-4e6b-8bde-31b84e15945f"],

Cell["\<\
Valuation shifts the digit positions without changing the period. This query \
avoids constructing a potentially enormous full-cycle integer.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"523e21da-caea-418f-a252-fbd499fb0c84"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Needs", "[", "\"\<PadicNumbers`\>\"", 
  "]"}]], "ExampleInitialization",ExpressionUUID->"c612f094-d9a0-4bea-a46b-\
156f7fa9ceaf"],

Cell["Basic Examples", "PrimaryExamplesSection",ExpressionUUID->"af5d3339-87c8-4809-b917-e48bc919fa5f"],

